1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,070
The plan was to drop the last commit,
the last saved snapshot on the branch.

2
00:00:09,110 --> 00:00:12,770
A hard reset moves the branch
back and overwrites your files.

3
00:00:12,810 --> 00:00:17,804
The typo was a 3 where a 1 was meant. Three
commits and their changes are gone from

4
00:00:17,844 --> 00:00:20,048
the branch and from the working tree.

5
00:00:20,388 --> 00:00:24,290
What the engineer does in the next
sixty seconds decides the outcome.

6
00:00:24,330 --> 00:00:29,088
One option is to search the web, find a
command that "cleans up the repository", and

7
00:00:29,128 --> 00:00:32,459
run it. Another is to delete
the directory and clone again.

8
00:00:32,499 --> 00:00:36,800
Both destroy the thing that would have
saved the work: the first removes the

9
00:00:36,840 --> 00:00:41,484
evidence, and the second produces a clone
with no reflog, Git's local log of where

10
00:00:41,524 --> 00:00:44,683
each name has pointed, and
none of the unpushed objects.

11
00:00:44,723 --> 00:00:47,654
The textbook calls re-cloning
"the classic non-fix".

12
00:00:47,994 --> 00:00:52,704
The correct first step is one word.
Stop. Keep those three commits in mind.

13
00:00:54,544 --> 00:00:58,020
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

14
00:00:58,060 --> 00:01:01,882
In the last two videos you learned
what Git keeps and for how long.

15
00:01:01,922 --> 00:01:06,436
Today that knowledge becomes a procedure
of eight steps, and you apply it three

16
00:01:06,476 --> 00:01:11,221
times: to a hard reset, to a deleted branch,
and to a commit that vanished from the

17
00:01:11,261 --> 00:01:12,316
middle of a branch.

18
00:01:12,656 --> 00:01:17,181
This is the guided pass. For each incident
I read the symptom, ask the three

19
00:01:17,221 --> 00:01:21,445
questions, show the evidence command,
then the anchor, then the repair.

20
00:01:21,485 --> 00:01:24,869
In the labs you repeat all
three from the symptoms alone.

21
00:01:25,209 --> 00:01:30,184
The curriculum summary for this video
describes the method in five words: stop,

22
00:01:30,224 --> 00:01:33,421
don't run maintenance, find
the ID, anchor, repair.

23
00:01:33,461 --> 00:01:38,563
The textbook's section 13.7 has eight
numbered steps, and that is the version you

24
00:01:38,603 --> 00:01:39,134
get here.

25
00:01:40,974 --> 00:01:44,403
ask the three questions that come
before any recovery command;

26
00:01:44,743 --> 00:01:47,986
recover the commits lost
to git reset --hard;

27
00:01:48,326 --> 00:01:52,421
recreate a deleted branch at its last
tip, with and without its reflog;

28
00:01:52,761 --> 00:01:54,870
bring back a commit dropped from history;

29
00:01:55,210 --> 00:01:58,885
anchor every recovered commit with
a ref before doing anything else.

30
00:02:00,725 --> 00:02:04,192
The three questions come from
the first page of the chapter.

31
00:02:04,232 --> 00:02:07,172
Was the work ever written
into the object database?

32
00:02:07,212 --> 00:02:10,152
Does anything still name
it? Has the clock run out?

33
00:02:10,192 --> 00:02:13,250
The first answer decides
whether Git can help at all.

34
00:02:13,290 --> 00:02:17,048
The second tells you the layer.
The third tells you the deadline.

35
00:02:17,388 --> 00:02:21,008
Then the method. Every recovery
follows the same eight steps.

36
00:02:27,348 --> 00:02:33,208
One. Stop. Don't run another command that
moves refs or deletes files, and don't run

37
00:02:33,248 --> 00:02:38,196
git gc, git prune, git clean or git
stash clear. Waiting loses nothing.

38
00:02:38,236 --> 00:02:39,530
A second "fix" can.

39
00:02:39,870 --> 00:02:44,858
Two. Record the symptom and the last
commands. Copy the terminal scrollback if you

40
00:02:44,898 --> 00:02:49,824
have it. Git prints the ID you need in
more places than people remember: "Deleted

41
00:02:49,864 --> 00:02:54,668
branch x (was ...)", "Dropped refs/stash@{0}",
"HEAD is now at", the warning on

42
00:02:54,708 --> 00:02:57,672
leaving detached HEAD,
when you are on no branch.

43
00:02:58,012 --> 00:03:02,749
Three. Classify. Was the work committed,
stashed, staged, or only saved?

44
00:03:02,789 --> 00:03:06,994
That decides the layer, and whether
to continue with Git at all.

45
00:03:07,334 --> 00:03:13,246
Four. Find the ID. From the scrollback,
the branch reflog, the HEAD reflog,

46
00:03:13,286 --> 00:03:17,848
ORIG_HEAD, git fsck, or another
repository, in that order.

47
00:03:18,188 --> 00:03:23,227
Five. Anchor it. git branch rescue/<what>
<id>. A branch is a name for a commit, and

48
00:03:23,267 --> 00:03:25,161
an anchored commit can't expire.

49
00:03:25,501 --> 00:03:30,702
Six. Inspect before you move anything.
git log, git show --stat, git diff, git

50
00:03:30,742 --> 00:03:33,524
range-diff, git cherry against the anchor.

51
00:03:33,864 --> 00:03:39,790
Seven. Integrate with the lowest-risk move.
Prefer adding, a branch, a cherry-pick

52
00:03:39,830 --> 00:03:45,465
or a merge, over rewinding. And prefer
git reset --keep over git reset --hard.

53
00:03:45,805 --> 00:03:51,162
Eight. Verify, then clean up. Prove that
the result holds what was lost, and only

54
00:03:51,202 --> 00:03:53,160
then delete the rescue branch.

55
00:03:53,500 --> 00:03:56,857
Why is the anchor a separate
step, before any repair?

56
00:03:56,897 --> 00:04:02,111
Because of what it costs and what it buys.
🟢 SAFE: git branch rescue/x <id> leaves

57
00:04:02,151 --> 00:04:06,405
the working tree, the index, HEAD
and the current branch unchanged.

58
00:04:06,445 --> 00:04:11,660
It adds one ref with a new reflog. In
exchange, the commit moves from layer two or

59
00:04:11,700 --> 00:04:15,569
three to layer one, and no expiry
or collection can touch it.

60
00:04:15,609 --> 00:04:18,837
After that, every repair
you attempt is reversible.

61
00:04:19,177 --> 00:04:22,841
When not to follow the method
to its end, from section 13.17.

62
00:04:22,881 --> 00:04:27,699
Don't recover a secret: if the "lost"
commit contains a credential, restoring it

63
00:04:27,739 --> 00:04:30,978
puts the credential back on
a branch, so rotate first.

64
00:04:31,018 --> 00:04:35,837
Don't rewind shared history to recover:
on a branch that others have pulled, add

65
00:04:35,877 --> 00:04:40,634
commits. And recovered isn't verified: a
branch that points at the right commit

66
00:04:40,674 --> 00:04:43,731
proves nothing about the
working tree you now have.

67
00:04:43,771 --> 00:04:44,581
Run the tests.

68
00:04:46,421 --> 00:04:50,940
Think of a recovery as moving a box in
the vault back under an index card.

69
00:04:50,980 --> 00:04:54,636
In all the accidents of today,
the box never left the vault.

70
00:04:54,676 --> 00:04:59,626
The commit never stopped existing. What
was lost was the card: the ref that named

71
00:04:59,666 --> 00:04:59,811
it.

72
00:05:00,151 --> 00:05:04,670
So every one of these recoveries ends
with the same act: a ref is pointed at a

73
00:05:04,710 --> 00:05:08,587
commit that never stopped existing.
The hard part isn't the repair.

74
00:05:08,627 --> 00:05:13,321
It's finding the right box number, and not
ordering a clear-out while you search.

75
00:05:13,661 --> 00:05:17,054
Where this picture breaks: it
covers committed work only.

76
00:05:17,094 --> 00:05:21,873
Uncommitted work that a hard reset overwrote
was never in a box, or was a single

77
00:05:21,913 --> 00:05:24,644
blob without a card.
That's a different video.

78
00:05:26,484 --> 00:05:30,476
Today's three incidents are three
branches of the "committed" subtree.

79
00:05:30,516 --> 00:05:35,258
Try it now, thirty seconds: point at the
branch of this tree for each of the three.

80
00:05:35,298 --> 00:05:36,467
Then say your answer.

81
00:05:41,307 --> 00:05:45,917
The hard reset: a branch moved. The
deleted branch: the branch itself is gone.

82
00:05:45,957 --> 00:05:49,077
The dropped commit: a branch
moved, through a rebase.

83
00:05:53,417 --> 00:05:58,123
"HEAD is now at 798cb66".
Three commits shorter.

84
00:05:58,463 --> 00:06:02,170
Stop, and ask the three questions.
Was the work an object?

85
00:06:02,210 --> 00:06:06,951
Yes, three commits. On the picture they
turn dashed, and they still exist.

86
00:06:06,991 --> 00:06:11,279
Does anything name it? Predict which
three places. Say it out loud.

87
00:06:11,319 --> 00:06:11,925
I'll wait.

88
00:06:16,765 --> 00:06:22,904
The HEAD reflog, ORIG_HEAD and the branch
reflog, and they agree: the old tip is

89
00:06:22,944 --> 00:06:23,522
495f3a9.

90
00:06:23,862 --> 00:06:29,335
ORIG_HEAD is safe to use here, because git
reset wrote it and nothing has run since.

91
00:06:29,375 --> 00:06:30,581
Anchor, look, move.

92
00:06:37,921 --> 00:06:42,517
The rescue branch lists all six
commits. The branch is back at 495f3a9.

93
00:06:42,557 --> 00:06:46,369
ORIG_HEAD moves as well, because
this reset wrote it again.

94
00:06:46,409 --> 00:06:50,809
The lost commits were on layer two,
the reflogs, for the whole time.

95
00:06:50,849 --> 00:06:53,747
Those are the three
commits from the opening.

96
00:06:54,087 --> 00:06:58,406
The usual complication, said now so that
you recognise it in the lab: if you

97
00:06:58,446 --> 00:07:03,109
committed new work on the shortened branch
before you noticed, a reset back to the

98
00:07:03,149 --> 00:07:07,869
old tip trades one loss for another. Anchor
both tips, reset to the one you want as

99
00:07:07,909 --> 00:07:10,909
the base, and cherry-pick
the commits from the other.

100
00:07:11,249 --> 00:07:14,086
The same slip without --hard is milder.

101
00:07:14,426 --> 00:07:19,253
A mixed reset moves the branch and the
index and leaves the working tree alone.

102
00:07:19,293 --> 00:07:23,751
The content of the two "missing" commits
is still in your files, shown as

103
00:07:23,791 --> 00:07:28,864
modifications. A second mixed reset to
HEAD@{1} puts the branch and the index back,

104
00:07:28,904 --> 00:07:30,343
and the status is clean.

105
00:07:30,683 --> 00:07:35,592
Git refused the safe form, -d, because
the branch was "not fully merged".

106
00:07:35,632 --> 00:07:39,456
That refusal is the first
safety net. -D skips the check.

107
00:07:39,496 --> 00:07:44,270
It is 🔴 DANGEROUS, because it deletes
the ref together with its reflog.

108
00:07:44,310 --> 00:07:47,930
It also prints the one fact
you need: "(was 2f65488)".

109
00:07:47,970 --> 00:07:50,913
Step two of the method:
copy the scrollback.

110
00:07:51,253 --> 00:07:55,154
On the picture, the label is gone
and three commits have no name.

111
00:07:55,194 --> 00:07:58,912
A branch is a ref, so recreating
the ref recreates the branch.

112
00:07:59,252 --> 00:08:03,212
Predict: how many lines will the
reflog of the recreated branch have?

113
00:08:03,552 --> 00:08:09,090
The three commits are back. The reflog isn't:
it has one line, "branch: Created from

114
00:08:09,130 --> 00:08:14,535
2f65488". The record of how the branch
moved over its life was deleted with it and

115
00:08:14,575 --> 00:08:15,731
can't be restored.

116
00:08:16,071 --> 00:08:19,271
On the picture, the label is back,
and the chain is solid again.

117
00:08:19,611 --> 00:08:25,179
And without the printed ID? The branch
reflog is gone, so ask the HEAD reflog.

118
00:08:25,519 --> 00:08:29,945
"checkout: moving from feature/cross-encoder
to main" records where HEAD went.

119
00:08:29,985 --> 00:08:34,698
The entry below it, HEAD@{2}, is the last
thing HEAD pointed at while it was on the

120
00:08:34,738 --> 00:08:39,509
branch. That's the tip, unless the branch
was moved afterwards without being checked

121
00:08:39,549 --> 00:08:43,288
out. In a long reflog, search for
the message instead of counting.

122
00:08:43,628 --> 00:08:48,026
Now the hard variant: no reflog knows the
commits. A teammate's branch that you

123
00:08:48,066 --> 00:08:52,689
fetched and never checked out is held by a
remote-tracking ref only, your record of

124
00:08:52,729 --> 00:08:53,981
a branch on the server.

125
00:08:54,321 --> 00:08:57,973
Git printed "(none)" where a
local deletion prints the old ID.

126
00:08:58,312 --> 00:09:03,056
The commits are on layer three
now. Quick quiz, two options.

127
00:09:03,096 --> 00:09:08,237
Does plain git fsck, without
--no-reflogs, find them: yes, or no?

128
00:09:08,277 --> 00:09:09,513
Say it out loud.

129
00:09:14,353 --> 00:09:18,273
Yes, without any option, because
no reflog entry competes.

130
00:09:18,313 --> 00:09:23,735
--not --all limits the listing to commits
that no ref reaches, which is the lost

131
00:09:23,775 --> 00:09:25,237
work and nothing else.

132
00:09:30,577 --> 00:09:33,825
Anchored, and pushed, which
puts it on layer four as well.

133
00:09:33,865 --> 00:09:38,530
In a repository with default settings this
rescue has a deadline: the grace period,

134
00:09:38,570 --> 00:09:43,065
counted from the day the objects were
written into your repository, and then the

135
00:09:43,105 --> 00:09:43,972
next collection.

136
00:09:44,312 --> 00:09:49,810
git log --all knows one commit for that
file. The timeout commit is on no branch.

137
00:09:49,850 --> 00:09:55,552
Commits leave a branch through three commands:
git reset, git rebase, and git commit

138
00:09:55,592 --> 00:09:59,176
--amend. The reflog of the
branch says which one ran.

139
00:09:59,516 --> 00:10:04,657
One "rebase (finish)" line, with the old
tip 76a49ed directly below it and the lost

140
00:10:04,697 --> 00:10:09,777
commit 2ebd700 two lines further down.
Anchor the old tip, and compare by content.

141
00:10:09,817 --> 00:10:15,021
git cherry marks with a plus the commits
whose change has no equivalent on the other

142
00:10:15,061 --> 00:10:15,333
side.

143
00:10:15,673 --> 00:10:19,721
Here's what that reflog describes.
A branch of four commits.

144
00:10:19,761 --> 00:10:24,422
Then a rebase, which copies commits:
it rebuilt two and left one out.

145
00:10:24,462 --> 00:10:29,396
Then one new commit on top. The old
ones turn dashed, 2ebd700 among them.

146
00:10:29,736 --> 00:10:33,909
Two commits were rebuilt with new
IDs and the same change: minus.

147
00:10:33,949 --> 00:10:35,400
One was left out: plus.

148
00:10:37,740 --> 00:10:41,580
Which repair? You could reset
the branch to the rescue branch.

149
00:10:41,620 --> 00:10:46,586
Look at the reflog again before you answer:
a commit was added after the rebase.

150
00:10:46,626 --> 00:10:50,841
A reset would lose it. Step seven
says prefer adding over rewinding.

151
00:10:57,181 --> 00:11:01,540
The setting is back, as a new commit on
top, and nothing after the rebase was

152
00:11:01,580 --> 00:11:04,454
disturbed. A cherry-pick
copied the change forward.

153
00:11:06,295 --> 00:11:07,943
Five mistakes to watch for.

154
00:11:08,283 --> 00:11:13,315
Running a clean-up command during the
incident. Root cause: git gc, git prune, git

155
00:11:13,355 --> 00:11:18,263
stash clear and git reflog expire remove
the names and objects that the recovery

156
00:11:18,303 --> 00:11:18,944
depends on.

157
00:11:19,284 --> 00:11:24,045
Deleting the repository and cloning again.
Root cause: reflogs are local and start

158
00:11:24,085 --> 00:11:27,734
with the clone, and the clone has
none of the unpushed objects.

159
00:11:28,074 --> 00:11:33,710
Using HEAD@{1} some commands later. Root
cause: any later movement, even a switch,

160
00:11:33,750 --> 00:11:38,416
shifts the positions; search with
--grep-reflog and use the full ID.

161
00:11:38,756 --> 00:11:43,762
Repairing before anchoring. Root cause:
a found commit is still unreachable from

162
00:11:43,802 --> 00:11:48,681
refs; until a branch names it, a failed
repair or an expiry can lose it again.

163
00:11:49,021 --> 00:11:53,553
Resetting a branch back to recover one
commit. Root cause: the reset discards

164
00:11:53,593 --> 00:11:57,115
commits made since; copy the
missing commit forward instead.

165
00:11:58,955 --> 00:12:03,474
Now, out of the lab. A search team keeps
an experiment branch on the server for

166
00:12:03,514 --> 00:12:08,032
hard-negative mining. Its author deletes
it after the experiment is written up.

167
00:12:08,072 --> 00:12:12,245
A week later a colleague needs two of
its commits for a new training run.

168
00:12:12,285 --> 00:12:17,034
He had fetched the branch and never checked
it out, and his nightly fetch runs with

169
00:12:17,074 --> 00:12:17,495
pruning.

170
00:12:17,835 --> 00:12:23,731
He classifies first: committed work, no
ref, no reflog entry in his clone, so layer

171
00:12:23,771 --> 00:12:29,451
three, with a deadline. He doesn't run
anything that might trigger a collection.

172
00:12:29,491 --> 00:12:35,387
git fsck --lost-found gives one dangling
commit. A log of that tip with --not --all

173
00:12:35,427 --> 00:12:38,748
shows exactly the two
commits and their author.

174
00:12:39,088 --> 00:12:43,931
He anchors the tip, pushes the branch so
that a second repository holds it, and only

175
00:12:43,971 --> 00:12:48,233
then tells the author that the experiment
is back under a new branch name.

176
00:12:48,273 --> 00:12:51,663
The incident note gives the
full commit ID, not a selector.

177
00:12:53,503 --> 00:13:00,913
Your turn. Do Lab 12.1, "An accidental hard
reset", in lab-manual/m12-recovery.md.

178
00:13:01,253 --> 00:13:05,868
Work from the symptom only. Before you
run an evidence command, write down the

179
00:13:05,908 --> 00:13:10,702
answers to the three questions and name
the three places that should hold the old

180
00:13:10,742 --> 00:13:15,477
tip. In the lab's failure scenario, new
work has been committed on the shortened

181
00:13:15,517 --> 00:13:20,371
branch. Predict what a plain reset to the
old tip would cost before you choose the

182
00:13:20,411 --> 00:13:20,789
repair.

183
00:13:21,129 --> 00:13:25,440
The challenge is Lab 12.2, "A
deleted branch", in the same file.

184
00:13:27,280 --> 00:13:28,546
Pause and answer out loud.

185
00:13:33,386 --> 00:13:38,141
A strong answer gives the three questions
in an order that narrows the search, and

186
00:13:38,181 --> 00:13:42,818
for each one says what a "no" means in
practice. It ties the answers to the four

187
00:13:42,858 --> 00:13:47,730
layers by name. It mentions the instruction
that precedes all three: what the person

188
00:13:47,770 --> 00:13:52,407
must not run while you think. And it ends
with what you'll say to the person who

189
00:13:52,447 --> 00:13:56,442
asked, in terms of confidence and
deadline, not in terms of commands.

190
00:13:58,282 --> 00:14:01,209
Let's land this. You
should now be able to say:

191
00:14:01,549 --> 00:14:06,368
Before any command I ask: was it an object,
does anything name it, has the clock run

192
00:14:06,408 --> 00:14:06,600
out.

193
00:14:06,940 --> 00:14:11,837
The method is stop, record, classify, find
the ID, anchor, inspect, integrate with

194
00:14:11,877 --> 00:14:13,583
the lowest-risk move, verify.

195
00:14:13,923 --> 00:14:18,805
After a hard reset the old tip is in
the branch reflog, the HEAD reflog and

196
00:14:18,845 --> 00:14:19,461
ORIG_HEAD.

197
00:14:19,801 --> 00:14:24,319
A deleted branch is recreated at its
tip; its own reflog is gone for good.

198
00:14:24,659 --> 00:14:29,332
A commit dropped by a rebase is copied
forward with a cherry-pick, not recovered by

199
00:14:29,372 --> 00:14:29,899
rewinding.

200
00:14:31,739 --> 00:14:38,688
Read section 13.7 and the first three
recoveries of section 13.8 in Chapter 13.

201
00:14:38,728 --> 00:14:44,261
Do Lab 12.3, "A deleted commit",
in lab-manual/m12-recovery.md.

202
00:14:44,601 --> 00:14:48,180
Today you brought back commits
three times with one method.

203
00:14:48,220 --> 00:14:52,290
Do the hard reset lab from the
symptom alone before the next video.

204
00:14:52,330 --> 00:14:56,952
Next time: five more recoveries, from
a wrong rebase to a wrong cherry-pick.

205
00:14:56,992 --> 00:15:00,142
Until then, look at the
state first and type second.

206
00:15:00,182 --> 00:15:01,614
See you in the next one.
